package net.portswigger.mcp.jobs

/** Shared public input contract; no dependency on repository documents. */
internal const val JOB_PROPOSAL_CONTRACT = """Proposal contract (JSON field names are exact; unknown fields are rejected): JobProposal={schemaVersion:1,jobId:string matching [A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,80},revision:positive integer,objective:nonblank string,targetPolicy:TargetPermit[],materials:HttpMaterial[],session:SessionPlan,dynamicBindings:DynamicBinding[] default [],variants:ScanVariant[],requestedInputParameters:InputParameter[] default [] (required for new proposals with explicit insertionPointParameters),storedVerificationSourceIds:string[] default [],requiredCapabilities:string[],cleanupPolicy:nonblank string,validationMaxAgeSeconds:integer 1..86400 default 3600,executionApprovalMaxAgeSeconds:integer 1..86400 default 3600,limitations:string[] default [],inputDigests:map of input path to SHA256 default {}}. Endpoint={scheme:"http"|"https",host:string,port:integer,path:origin-form path including optional query}. TargetPermit={endpoint:Endpoint,pathPrefix:boolean default false}; all material, diagnostic, preparationOnce, branch, Macro and CPH destinations must be covered. HttpMaterial={id:unique string,endpoint,request:full raw string,response:full raw string,requestSha256,responseSha256}; hashes are lower-case SHA256 of UTF-8 encoding of the exact strings, not the binary wire hash. For non-UTF8 bodies use the native requestBase64 observation and a lossless byte-to-Latin1 raw string, never replacement characters. RequestPlan={endpoint,request,sourceId,sourceRequestSha256,startOptions:object default {}}; sourceId must identify observed material and sourceRequestSha256 must equal that material's requestSha256. A prepared request can differ from its source but must preserve intended inputs and be independently reviewed. Only the diagnostic variant request may set startOptions (insertionPointParameters,payloadProfile,etaProfile,workload, with the existing start_active_scan field formats). EVERY branchSetup RequestPlan MUST omit startOptions or use {}; copying diagnostic scan selectors into a branch is rejected with BRANCH_SCAN_OPTIONS_FORBIDDEN. Branches send workflow tests, not additional scans.
SessionPlan={authenticationRequired:boolean,validationDecision:"USE"|"OMIT",decisionReason:nonblank string,accountRef:nonblank string,secretGenerations:map default {},validationSourceId:optional material id}. First assess the actual target immediately before creating settings. A replayable GET/HEAD with observed valid matching credentials and accepted unmodified controls may support a standalone Scan without Macro/CPH/Rule; do not add helpers solely because it is authenticated. Explain the observed reuse/expiry behavior and inspect post-scan controls: current validity does not prove coverage for the entire Audit. When continuing authentication needs validation/recovery, use USE with the existing generate_session_validation_action and an observed read-only GET/HEAD validationSourceId using the same effective credentials. Reuse the owned session while valid, recover login only when missing/invalid, and keep stop_if_valid=false so CSRF/data preparation and stored verification still run. Do not justify OMIT by a self-imposed Logout on every request. Login/logout diagnostics and observed session-invalidating operations keep their necessary isolation; explain OMIT from those observed requirements. When validation/recovery is needed, missing safe probe or recovery evidence is a readiness gap, not evidence that validation is unnecessary. Keep per-request CPH sources fresh when login is skipped and after settings reapplication; never overwrite attacked inputs or assume a previous login-response cache was refreshed. For ordinary business workflows with validationDecision=USE, required branches must cover valid/no-login and invalid-session/recovery using correct recovery credentials, with observed branchSetup for non-normal branches and at least five semantic checks each; verify actual login counts. This is not a wrong-password requirement and does not require additional branches for isolated login diagnostics using OMIT. Separate final owned-session Logout from per-request business cleanup, perform it after final variants/benign controls via an approved cleanup job when needed, and never confuse settings restoration with application-session invalidation. Native validation has a binary match: timeout/5xx/ambiguous responses must not be reported as proven expiry or successful maintenance; verify failure handling and do not blindly replay uncertain mutations. DynamicBinding={id,source,destination,extraction,insertion,timing,failurePolicy:nonblank strings,dependsOn:string[] default []}; dependency ids must precede the binding. These describe the native settings, not an executable substitute for them. ScanVariant={id:string matching [A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,80},kind:"REFLECTION"|"STORED",request:RequestPlan,settings:SettingsPlan,requiredBranches:unique string[] default ["normal"],branchSetup:map from branch name to RequestPlan[] default {},verificationSourceId:optional material id,inputExclusions:InputExclusion[] default [],preparationOnce:RequestPlan[] default []}. Exactly one REFLECTION variant is required; every non-normal required branch needs branchSetup. Each STORED variant must identify one observed verification source, and their verificationSourceIds must exactly cover storedVerificationSourceIds. REFLECTION preserves the diagnostic response; STORED uses the final post-Macro response for the current stored effect and cannot append Logout after that selected response. Review cleanup of the last stored session separately.
InputParameter={name:exact native parameter name,type:exact case-sensitive native type}. requestedInputParameters independently declares the inputs required by the user; copy the actual requested scope before choosing selectors, never derive this declaration from a narrowed allowlist. New selective proposals must declare this nonempty list of at most 128 identities without duplicates, and EVERY variant must explicitly select exactly that entire requested set. Missing declarations, omitted requested inputs, additional selected inputs or an automatic variant in a declared selective plan are refused before approval or target traffic. inputExclusions cannot remove a requested input even with a reason, a future-audit promise or coverage in another variant/job. Change requested scope only on an explicit user scope change, never infer permission to split or defer it. This verifies consistency of declared intent and plan; the server does not read the conversation or authenticate the natural-language scope. Show the independent requested inputs and per-variant selection to the human reviewer. The empty default preserves old saved-plan decoding and unchanged automatic-selection plans. InputExclusion={name:exact native parameter name,type:exact case-sensitive native type,reason:nonblank string at most 1000 characters}. For explicit insertionPointParameters, formal registration uses the same native parser as Scan without sending HTTP: every native parameter (including Cookie parameters) must be selected or explicitly excluded with a reason. Names/types must exist; selected/excluded sets must not overlap. Do not silently exclude CSRF, nonce, session state or another input because a Macro updates it. Show exclusions to the operator and explain the requested scope. Unsupported/encoded/opaque bodies refuse selective coverage rather than pretending a partial inventory is complete. A parameter-updating pre-Macro or conditional recovery Macro must not overwrite any selected input; automatic selection is checked conservatively against native parameters when such actions apply. Separate state acquisition from attack input preservation while retaining the entire requested set in each configuration. An intentional attack on a CSRF/token value may correctly return a token error; that response alone is not a configuration failure and is not a reason to remove the selected token. Distinguish it from a stale token or failed authentication in a normal control or prerequisite. Assess observed token/cookie binding and reuse or expiry: when a previously observed matched pair remains valid, retaining that pair without Macro updates can preserve normal controls and attacked token values in one configuration. Prove normal acceptance and payload preservation; never assume all tokens are reusable, invent a pair, or blindly refresh an attacked value. These checks do not prove arbitrary CPH transformations, header/path coverage, actual Task strategy or the truth of an exclusion reason; keep wire verification and native insertion-point review. Static drafts check declarations and explicit Macro conflicts but defer native inventory completeness to formal registration.
preparationOnce is for observed, authorized one-time recovery or setup traffic. It runs once per variant during prepare, after that variant's settings are applied and before the five-per-branch workflow probes. It never runs inside the Scanner callback or execute and does not count toward the five tests. Each entry must have an observed source/hash, permitted endpoint and empty startOptions. Use it for old owned-session cleanup instead of embedding fixed old SID Logout/check pairs in every-request Macros. Repeated Macros that disable request-cookie inheritance and revoke a literal nonempty Cookie are rejected; use proven dynamic carry-forward for per-request Logout. Verify ownership and each one-time response separately; a received response alone is not application success. Evidence is durable and bound into validationDigest. Unknown outcomes require inspection, not automatic replay. Do not rely on one-time runtime caches surviving settings restoration/reapplication; per-request prerequisites remain Macro/CPH responsibilities.
SettingsPlan={macros:native JSON array default [],rules:native JSON array default [],cph:native config object default {},cphOrder:string[] default [],targetScope:optional native scope object,macroPreparations:MacroPreparation[] default [],serialScannerRequests:boolean default false,authenticationStates:optional AuthenticationStates}. Obtain full native formats and current definitions with get_macros/get_macro_detail/get_session_handling_rules/get_cph_config/output_project_options and the existing generate_* configuration tools; retain native fields, integer serial references and enabled state, do not substitute abbreviated settings. New Macro/Rule descriptions and CPH names use ownership prefix job:<jobId>:; use unique non-colliding positive integer Macro serials. CPH tab order includes every resulting tab (including Options and existing definitions) in intended order. MacroPreparation={macroSerialNumber:integer,itemIndex:zero-based integer,sourceId,sourceRequestSha256,substitutions:[{before:string,after:string}] default [],cphTrigger:optional string}; use only explicit observed-source substitutions and corresponding CPH trigger preparation. Do not fabricate HTTP observations. Dynamic native workflows require DYNAMIC_WORKFLOW_VERIFIED; requiredCapabilities must include SCANNER_WORKFLOW and EXCLUSIVE_CONTEXT and only installed supported capabilities. Preserve full source responses even when an unused native setting uses its default. Snapshot every referenced payload workbook/catalog in inputDigests. A compiler success is structural validation, not proof that authentication, rejection paths or cleanup work.
JobProposal additionally accepts authenticationRetention:optional AuthenticationRetention={ruleDescriptions:nonempty unique string[],cphNames:unique string[] default [],retainTargetScope:boolean default false}. Select newly added reusable authentication Rules by their exact job:<jobId>: description; each must be enabled and contain an enabled validate_session action. Referenced validation/recovery Macros are retained automatically by integer references, including nested references. Explicitly list EVERY CPH handler needed by those Macros/Rules (including trigger cleanup and dynamic extraction); cached_selection dependencies are retained automatically. Existing shared Options is restored to its original baseline after each stage. The server cannot infer arbitrary regex/extension dependencies: the AI must inspect them and verify the whole workflow. When retaining CPH that uses added Target scope, set retainTargetScope=true; scope persistence must be shown in review. The compiler checks every variant with the shared retained definitions available. Retained new Rules/CPH stay in Burp disabled between targets; Macro definitions and all Check session is valid action fields remain intact. Temporary Rules/Macros/resource pools restore to the original baseline; unretained CPH residue remains disabled and reported. After successful preparation, execution completion and approved stop recovery preserve this retained baseline. Failed/incomplete preparation recovers the original baseline. This keeps definitions, not a valid login or runtime cache; do not assume settings reapplication refreshes tokens. Omission preserves legacy restoration/digests and never retroactively changes saved jobs.
AuthenticationStates={rules:map of exact existing baseline Rule description to boolean default {},cph:map of exact existing baseline CPH handler name to boolean default {},cphTools:unique case-sensitive string[] default []}. For EACH target the AI first decides whether a standalone Scan suffices, then explicitly enables needed reusable authentication Rules/CPH and disables unneeded ones in the variant settings. For every reused CPH handler enabled by this map, declare the tools that actually require it in cphTools: Scanner, Extender, Proxy, Target, Spider, Repeater, Sequencer or Intruder. Include Scanner for Scanner-side extraction/replacement and Extender for MCP/Macro prerequisite traffic when needed; do not assume an Extender probe proves Scanner processing. Missing scope is refused before settings or HTTP changes. The service enables the declared Options tool flags in the reviewed configuration, preserves all other global flags, and restores the original Options afterward. Disable-only or Rule-only selections need no cphTools. Native new configurations must include their observed complete Options; retention preserves their effective tool scope and original Rule execution order. Existing saved selections without required scope remain readable/recoverable but cannot receive new approval, prepare or start until revised. These maps change only enabled flags; they cannot create missing definitions, change Macro/action fields, or switch the shared Options tab. Existing Rules must contain validate_session. For newly defined native settings use their native enabled field instead of these baseline-only selectors. Check current names/scopes and cache dependencies with read tools, and include enabled cache sources when required. Do not enable every saved profile or run login unconditionally. Keep Check session is valid and conditional invalid-session recovery, and validate actual no-login/recovery branches and attack-input preservation under the final configuration. All switching is part of the reviewed managed plan and its five-per-required-branch tests, never a direct API/UI settings bypass. At restoration existing definitions return to their baseline enabled states, and newly retained definitions remain disabled for AI selection on a later target.
"""
